    pr_ref:  # 788c
    description: "A `modifiers` field on symbols carries language-specific visibility terms. Different language analyzers emit different vocabularies for what is semantically the same concept (whether a symbol is exported / publicly visible): one language emits negative markers only, several emit positive markers only, and a separate boolean field (`supply_chain.is_exported`) captures visibility from a path-based heuristic. The two encodings can contradict on a per-symbol basis, with the path-based heuristic disagreeing with the language-syntactic visibility on a small set of cases.\n\n### Specific symptoms confirmed on hypergumbo self-analysis\n\n**(1) `modifiers` vocabulary leaks 5 distinct language-specific visibility terms.** 4001 of 33830 nodes have a non-empty `modifiers` list. Five distinct values appear: `private` (3942 from Python), `re_exported` (39 from Python), `public` (8 from C# + Java), `exported` (6 from Go), `pub` (4 from Rust), `external` (2 from Solidity). Five different words for what is semantically \"visibility.\" Each language analyzer emits its own native visibility keyword without normalization to a shared vocabulary. A consumer asking \"is this symbol exported\" needs per-language polarity-aware logic to interpret the field.\n\n**(2) Asymmetric emission polarity: Python emits negative only, all other languages positive only.** Python: 3942 nodes have `modifiers=['private']`; 0 nodes have `public`/`exported`. C# / Java: 8 nodes have `['public']`; 0 have `private`. Go: 6 nodes have `['exported']`. Rust: 4 have `['pub']`. Solidity: 2 have `['external']`. A consumer cannot uniformly ask \"is this symbol exported\" across languages: for Python they check absence-of-private; for others they check presence-of-public/pub/exported. The `supply_chain.is_exported` boolean (already separate, 25% true overall) is the proper unified field — but it sits alongside this inconsistent `modifiers` field as a conceptual duplicate.\n\n**(3) `modifiers` and `supply_chain.is_exported` disagree on 7 specific nodes.** 5 Python functions have `modifiers=['private']` AND `supply_chain.is_exported=True`: `_controller_to_view_dir`, `_is_action_method`, `_camel_to_snake`, `_count_controller_methods`, `_probe_template_files` — all underscore-prefixed (so Python's analyzer tags them `private`) but in `packages/*/src/` paths (so the supply_chain path-heuristic tags them exported). 2 Solidity functions have `modifiers=['external']` AND `supply_chain.is_exported=False`: `IToken.transfer`, `Token.transfer` — both in `tests/` paths (so the supply_chain path-heuristic overrides the language-level `external` modifier). The `supply_chain.reason` field shows the conflict source: the path-based heuristic `'path matches ^packages/[^/]+/src/'` overrides language-level visibility, and `'test path matches (?:^|/)tests?/'` overrides the Solidity `external` modifier. Both are path-mediated overrides of language-mediated visibility; neither dominates.\n\n### Invariant\n\nVisibility should have a single canonical encoding with explicit semantics. Either the path-based heuristic is canonical and the language-specific `modifiers` set documents only its polarity (and consumers consult one field), or the `modifiers` vocabulary is unified across languages so that \"is this symbol exported\" is uniformly answerable from `modifiers` alone. The two encodings should not be allowed to silently disagree; a consumer asking visibility should get one consistent answer.\n\n### Scope\n\nAffects every symbol in languages that emit modifier values (4001 of 33830 nodes — about 12% of total). The cross-field contradictions concentrate on a small number of cases (7 observed) where the path-based heuristic and the language-syntactic visibility disagree.\n\n### Exposure\n\nBehavior-map per-symbol `modifiers` field and per-symbol `supply_chain.is_exported` field.\n\n### Consequence\n\nA consumer asking \"is this symbol exported\" needs per-language polarity-aware logic and must choose which encoding to trust when they disagree. Cross-language aggregations on visibility break — e.g., counting \"exported Python functions\" using the Python convention is incompatible with counting \"exported Solidity functions\" using the Solidity convention. The 7 known contradictions silently propagate to any consumer that joins the two fields.\n\n### Candidate fixes\n\n- Promote `supply_chain.is_exported` (or a successor field) as the canonical \"is exported\" answer; deprecate or document the per-language `modifiers` set as language-syntax-only and not a cross-language visibility axis.\n- Alternatively, normalize the `modifiers` vocabulary: define one cross-language set (e.g., `public`, `private`, `protected`) and have each language analyzer map its native terms onto it at emission time.\n- For the 7 contradictions, define a precedence rule: e.g., path-heuristic wins (or language-syntax wins, or contradiction emits a warning). Document the rule in the schema.\n- Add a property test that asserts no node has `modifiers` containing a \"private-ish\" term while `supply_chain.is_exported=True` (or define the inverse).\n\n### Subsumed (do not chase)\n\nThis item subsumes INV-fulov, INV-roluv, INV-vagaj — which are deleted or will be deleted shortly and should only be referenced in an emergency."  # 788c
    fields:  # 788c
      dogfood_anon_id: "ITEM-a7325f"  # 788c
      statement: "Visibility should have a single canonical encoding with explicit semantics. Either the path-based heuristic is canonical and the language-specific modifier set documents its polarity, or the modifier vocabulary is unified across languages. The two encodings should not be allowed to silently disagree."  # 788c
      root_cause: "A `modifiers` field on symbols carries language-specific visibility terms. Different language analyzers emit different vocabularies for what is semantically the same concept (whether a symbol is exported / publicly visible): one language emits `private` only (never positive markers), several emit only positive markers (`public`, `pub`, `exported`, `external`) and never negative ones. A separate boolean field captures visibility from a path-based heuristic. The two encodings of the same concept can contradict on a per-symbol basis, with the path-based heuristic disagreeing with the language-syntactic visibility on a small set of cases. Cases include underscore-prefixed private functions inside source paths that the heuristic marks as exported, and tests-directory symbols carrying the language's external marker that the heuristic marks as non-exported."  # 788c
